WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.200
Quantum machine learning.

2
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:06.167
What is the real mechanism, and what does it actually deliver today?

3
00:00:07.367 --> 00:00:11.033
Today, QML almost always means a variational circuit.

4
00:00:11.333 --> 00:00:14.867
First, classical data is encoded into a quantum state.

5
00:00:15.167 --> 00:00:18.900
Then a circuit of trainable rotations, called the ansatz, runs.

6
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:20.267
We measure a number.

7
00:00:20.567 --> 00:00:25.667
And a classical optimizer on your CPU updates the angles, then the loop repeats.

8
00:00:26.867 --> 00:00:29.100
Encoding decides everything downstream.

9
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:32.266
Basis encoding maps bits to basis states.

10
00:00:32.266 --> 00:00:33.733
Simple, but wasteful.

11
00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:37.599
Angle encoding turns each feature into a rotation.

12
00:00:37.599 --> 00:00:40.167
Almost every practical demo uses it.

13
00:00:40.467 --> 00:00:47.233
Amplitude encoding is compact, but preparing the state takes exponential time, which cancels the advantage.

14
00:00:48.433 --> 00:00:52.667
You cannot backpropagate through hardware, because measuring destroys the state.

15
00:00:52.967 --> 00:00:54.906
So we use the parameter shift rule.

16
00:00:54.906 --> 00:00:57.233
Run the circuit with the angle shifted up.

17
00:00:57.533 --> 00:00:59.100
Run it again shifted down.

18
00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:00.559
Subtract and halve.

19
00:01:00.559 --> 00:01:03.367
It is exact, but costs two runs per parameter.

20
00:01:04.567 --> 00:01:06.533
Here is the shape of it in PennyLane.

21
00:01:06.833 --> 00:01:09.000
Angle embedding encodes the features.

22
00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:11.800
Strongly entangling layers are the ansatz.

23
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:14.867
And a classical Adam optimizer does the training.

24
00:01:16.067 --> 00:01:18.382
Three problems keep QML in the lab.

25
00:01:18.382 --> 00:01:20.300
The first is barren plateaus.

26
00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:26.350
As circuits grow, gradients flatten exponentially, so the optimizer has nothing to follow.

27
00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:32.867
The other two are data loading, and dequantization, where classical algorithms match claimed speedups.

28
00:01:34.067 --> 00:01:35.200
The honest verdict.

29
00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:43.500
As of twenty twenty six, there is no demonstrated quantum advantage on any practical machine learning task with classical data.

30
00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:47.600
Quantum should matter first for simulating chemistry and materials.

31
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:49.665
Let's recap.

32
00:01:49.665 --> 00:01:54.133
QML is a variational circuit trained by a classical optimizer.

33
00:01:54.433 --> 00:01:56.633
Gradients come from the parameter shift rule.

34
00:01:56.933 --> 00:01:59.833
Data loading and barren plateaus block progress.

35
00:02:00.133 --> 00:02:04.551
Quantum inspired tensor networks already work on classical hardware.

36
00:02:04.551 --> 00:02:06.500
Next up: a three weekend plan.
